{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.TiltDerivativeCovariance","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TiltDerivativeCovariance","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Derivative","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tilt_deriv_ne_wCov_of_mean_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tilt_deriv_ne_wCov_of_mean_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L113-L124","doc":"**The vanishing-mean hypothesis is load-bearing.** A two-point family with\n`wMean ≠ 0` on which `tilt_deriv_eq_wCov`'s conclusion is FALSE. Without this, the\nhypothesis `hf` could be inert and the theorem above would be weaker than it reads. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum_eval_one_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum_eval_one_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L102-L107","doc":"The tilted mean vanishes at `x = 1` exactly when `f` has vanishing weighted mean —\nso `R(1) = 0` is not an extra assumption, it is the same hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tilt_deriv_eq_wCov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tilt_deriv_eq_wCov","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L88-L100","doc":"**The tilt derivative is the covariance.**\n\n`R'(1) = Cov_w(h, f)`, given only that `f` has vanishing `w`-weighted mean. In the\napplication `f = ω₃`, `h = Σ□h` and `w` is the Plancherel weight `(f^λ)²`, so this says\n`M₁` is the slope at `x = 1` of the `Σ□h`-tilted mean of `ω₃` — a function that\n*vanishes* at `x = 1`, which is exactly why the limit-shape evaluation there is\nuninformative and the derivative is not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.wCov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.wCov","kind":"def","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L84-L86","doc":"The `w`-weighted covariance of `g` and `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.wMean","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.wMean","kind":"def","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L80-L82","doc":"The `w`-weighted mean of `g`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum_derivative_eval_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum_derivative_eval_one","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L68-L72","doc":"The derivative of the tilted numerator at `x = 1` is the `h`-weighted first moment\nof `f`. This is the algebraic core: differentiating in the tilt brings down `hᵢ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tiltDen_eval_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tiltDen_eval_one","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L64-L66","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum_eval_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum_eval_one","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L60-L62","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tiltDen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tiltDen","kind":"def","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L57-L58","doc":"The tilted partition function `D(x) = ∑ᵢ wᵢ x^{hᵢ}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tiltNum","kind":"def","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TiltDerivativeCovariance.lean#L54-L55","doc":"The tilted numerator `N(x) = ∑ᵢ wᵢ fᵢ x^{hᵢ}`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}